# netsurf: a form's named controls shadow its built-ins (patch 297)
# Generated 2026-10-14
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 296)
#
# Patch 288's "not done", recorded in its Doc 10 entry: named access
# on a form only reached names HTMLFormElement.prototype does not
# have. The layer sits above the prototype, so a lookup for a name
# the prototype owns -- submit, action, length, the classic
# accidents a page ships when a field is named after one -- found
# the built-in first, where a browser's [LegacyOverrideBuiltIns]
# named-properties object sits between the instance and the
# prototype and the control wins: <input name="submit"> leaves
# form.submit the method here, and form.submit() from page code
# then submits when the page meant to read its field (or throws on
# the string it does get).
#
# The prototype's own members now answer through the named access.
# Each own descriptor of HTMLFormElement.prototype is wrapped where
# it sits -- a data property keeps its place and answers its value,
# an accessor keeps a getter and setter of its own; the wrapped
# getter asks the form's controls first, and the wrapped setter
# swallows a write to a shadowed name (the control reads back, as
# in browsers), keeps an unshadowed attribute's original setter,
# and otherwise takes an own property on the form. The bridge puts
# some of the form's own IDL attributes (action, method, target,
# name) on the shared element prototype above the form's, so those
# are wrapped where they live too; the form-only guard leaves every
# other element's reads exactly as they were. A reentrancy guard
# keeps the lookup's own reads (ownerDocument and friends, which
# the control search itself needs) out of the wrapping.
#
# Measured while doing it, not fixed here (they are absences, not
# shadowing): form.reset, form.enctype and form.encoding do not
# exist in the bridge at all -- a control named reset "shadows" a
# method that was never there. Controls named after the wider
# HTMLElement members (id, title, ...) also still do not shadow;
# the form's own interface is covered, the whole chain is not.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-form-shadow.html (FORMSHADOW 6/6 in the
# simulator and in a reference browser -- Firefox/Camoufox, for
# the reason patch 292's header gives). JS-form-named.html stays
# FORMNAMED 7/7. Simulator only; the vita cross-build was not run
# (no vitaSDK on this VM). Device expectation is in Doc 10.
#
# Decline conditions: if the bridge ever grows a real
# named-properties object in C (a per-interface prototype between
# instance and HTMLFormElement.prototype), this wrapping should be
# deleted in its favour.
#
diff --git a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/vita_polyfill.js b/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/vita_polyfill.js
--- a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/vita_polyfill.js
+++ b/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/vita_polyfill.js
